WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
Good to have you on Planetary and Daily, episode 68 coming to you on May 24th, 2026.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.720
Let's see what the latest research is telling us. Looking back at three episodes from

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:21.460
2026-05-18 to 2026-05-24, the stories that mattered, what we learned and what to watch next.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:27.200
A review published in Science Magazine connects several sleep processes directly to pathways

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:33.240
involved in dementia development. The analysis links glimphatic clearance of brain waste with

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.600
blood volume shifts heart rate variability and neuromodulator activity during sleep.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:44.660
Disrupted sleep impairs these integrated systems rather than any single factor in isolation.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:51.360
My NetterGuard describes how the combined physiological mechanism support waste removal in the brain.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:57.240
Next steps will focus on targeted sleep interventions tested in populations that elevated

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:04.440
risk for dementia. From brain waste clearance during sleep, we turn to another ancient biological system

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:10.700
that still shapes human health today. Long-running microbial competition has left lasting marks on

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:17.820
the architecture of human immune genes. Comparative genomic analysis across species in time shows how

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:23.800
historical pathogen pressures continue to influence modern immune responses. The findings appear

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.880
in Science Magazine and detail specific changes in immune gene structure shaped by ancient conflicts.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:36.020
These evolutionary traces affect how current immune systems recognize and respond to pathogens.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.020
The work highlights mechanisms preserved through deep time rather than recent adaptations alone.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:48.280
While immune defenses evolved over deep time, clinicians still need practical reference data for

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:54.020
everyday tissue assessment. Researchers at Charles University used ultrasound to measure deep

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:59.780
fascia thickness in the legs of healthy children. The resulting data established the first baseline

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:05.620
values for normal fascia thickness in this age group. These reference standards allow earlier,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.300
non-invasive detection of musculoskeletal conditions through simple comparisons.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:16.160
Doctors can now identify deviations from expected thickness without invasive procedures.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:21.500
Plans call for validation of the measurements across larger age groups in follow-up studies.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.340
From human tissue standards, we shift to standards of a different kind,

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:33.280
monitoring recovery in ocean populations. More than 60 years of citing records from Namibia and

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.180
South Africa's West Coast show most blue and thin whale observations occurred after 2012.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:45.820
The compilation published in the African Journal of Marine Science reports 95% of confirmed

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:51.820
sightings and strandings in that later period. Absolute numbers for both species remained low

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:57.260
decades after the end of commercial whaling. The data leave open whether the rise reflects

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:03.380
population recovery or redistribution of existing animals. Continued monitoring will clarify the

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:09.100
underlying cause of the trend. From large-scale environmental monitoring, we move to precise

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:15.860
non-contact measurement at the nanoscale. A German Israeli team, led by Dr. Andreas Furtner,

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:22.320
applied two complementary ellipsumontri methods to microstructured MXCN thin films.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.360
The approach maps uniformity and integrity across the film during fabrication steps that include

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:34.760
lithography. Measurements occur without contacting or altering the material itself. Results appear

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:40.720
as an editor's pick in applied physics letters. From contact-free materials inspection, we look at

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:46.980
contact-free sensing of an entirely different sort. Everyday Wi-Fi signals can distinguish individual

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:53.300
people with near-perfect accuracy even when devices are powered off. The method relies on subtle

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:59.340
radio-frequency reflections generated by the human body. Tests demonstrated reliable identification

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:05.860
under standard indoor conditions without any active device participation. Privacy implications arise

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:11.760
immediately from the passive nature of the sensing. From contact-free sensing of an entirely different

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:18.200
sort, we turn to quantum optics that now operates without specialized equipment. Ambient sunlight alone

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:24.260
can now generate photon pairs suitable for high-quality ghost imaging. The technique removes the

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:30.480
requirement for dedicated laser sources in quantum optics setups. Experiments produced clear images

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:36.460
using only natural sunlight as the illumination source. Potential applications extend to both

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:42.520
imaging systems and broader quantum optics experiments. That's Planetarian Daily for today.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:48.740
If you enjoyed this, a rating or review on Apple Podcasts or Spotify really helps new listeners find

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:54.800
the show. I'm Patrick and Vancouver, thanks for listening, and I'll see you tomorrow. This episode

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:00.200
used AI voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
